.he "dc (1)                       9/30/26                       dc (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
dc
- desk calculator
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
dc [ file ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Dc
is an arbitrary precision desk calculator: integers of any length
and fractions to a scale you set, all exact.  It reads the standard
input and writes the standard output, and a file named on the command
line is read first and the standard input after it.
.sp
The calculator is a stacking, or reverse Polish, one.  A number is
pushed on the stack as it is read, and an operator takes what it
needs from the top of the stack and pushes back what it makes.  So
.bd
2 100 ^ p
prints all thirty-one digits of two to the hundredth, and
.bd
3 k 1 3 / p
prints a third to three places.
.sp
A number is a string of digits, an underscore to make it negative,
and a decimal point if it has a fraction.  In bases above ten the
letters A to F are digits as well.
.sp
.nf
.in +5
12
_12
12.5
_12.5
.in -5
.fi
.sp
Those are numbers and nothing else is: a minus sign between two of
them is the subtraction operator and not a sign.
.sp
The operators are
.bd
+ - * / % ^
which add, subtract, multiply, divide, take the remainder, and raise
the second value to the power of the top.  The top value is the right
operand, so two three minus is minus one.  An exponent must be a
whole number, and is refused if it is very large; a negative one
divides into one.  A fractional part of an exponent is ignored.
.sp
The scale is the number of decimal places a division keeps.  It is
set by
.bd
k
from the top of the stack and pushed back by
.bd
K
, which reports it.  A division keeps exactly the scale and throws
away the rest.  A multiplication keeps the sum of the scales of its
two operands, or the scale itself where the scale is larger.
Addition and subtraction keep the larger of the two scales.
.sp
An input base and an output base are set the same way by
.bd
i
and
.bd
o
, and read back by
.bd
I
and
.bd
O
, which push the value each holds.  An input base applies to numbers
read after it and an output base to numbers printed after it, so
.sp
.in +5
.ti +5
.bd
16 o 243 p
.br
.in -5
.sp
prints F3.  An output base from two to sixteen uses one character for
each digit; above sixteen each digit is printed in decimal, which is
how this dc has always printed the larger bases.
.sp
The remaining commands are these.
.sp
.in +10
.ta 8
.ti -10
.bd
s x
.br
Save.  The top of the stack is popped into the register named x,
which is one character.  A capital
.bd
S
treats that register as a stack and pushes the value on it instead.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
l x
.br
Load.  A copy of the value in register x is pushed on the stack; the
register keeps it.  A register never written reads as zero.  A
capital
.bd
L
pops the register's stack instead.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
d
.br
Duplicate the top of the stack.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
c
.br
Clear.  Every value on the stack is discarded.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
p
.br
Print the top of the stack, which is left there.  A capital
.bd
P
takes the top of the stack to be a character string, removes it, and
prints it without a newline.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
f
.br
Print every value on the stack, the top one first.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
v
.br
Replace the top of the stack by its square root.  A negative one is
an error.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
z
.br
Push the number of values on the stack.  A capital
.bd
Z
replaces the top value with its length in digits, and a capital
.bd
X
with its scale.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
q
.br
Quit.  Inside a string the two levels of execution the string was
running at are dropped instead.  A capital
.bd
Q
pops the top of the stack and drops that many levels.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
x
.br
Execute the top of the stack as a string of dc commands.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
[ ... ]
.br
Put the bracketed string on the top of the stack.  Brackets pair, so
a string may hold another.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
< x
,
.bd
> x
,
.bd
= x
.br
Compare the top two values and execute register x if they are less
than, greater than, or equal to one another.  The comparison is the
second value against the top, so two one
.bd
> x
does not fire and one two
.bd
> x
does.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
; r
,
.bd
: r
.br
Arrays.  The top of the stack is an index and the register named
after the command holds an array of values.  Semicolon loads the
indexed element; colon stores the value below the index into it.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
!
.br
Run the rest of the line as a shell command.
.sp
.ti -10
.bd
?
.br
Read a line from the input and execute it as dc commands.
.in -10
.sp
The scale, the input base and the output base are best changed
together; a value read in one base and printed in another is
converted through the scale and can surprise.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
Print the first ten factorials:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
[la1+dsa*pla10>y]sy
0sa1
lyx
.in -5
.fi
.sp
Dc has no loops of its own; the string in register y is the body of
one, and it calls itself while the counter in register a is still
below ten.
.sp
What is 243 in hexadecimal?
.sp
.nf
.in +5
16 o 243 p
.in -5
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
Every message is printed on the standard output and dc reads the next
command, so a bad line in a long input does not end it.
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
num is unimplemented	A character that is no command, printed in
octal.
.sp
.ti -10
stack empty	There were not enough values on the stack to do what
was asked.
.sp
.ti -10
divide by 0	The divisor was zero.
.sp
.ti -10
out of space, out of stack space, symbol table overflow	Memory or
stack exhausted.
.sp
.ti -10
index too big, neg index	An array index out of range.
.sp
.ti -10
exp too big, exp not an integer, scale too big, sqrt of neg number	A
value an operator cannot work with.
.sp
.ti -10
nesting depth, readstk?, Q?, neg Q, L?, save: args	The string and
register stacks used past their ends.
.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is v7's dc, the whole desk calculator, and it is a much larger
program than the dc that was in /bin on the 1.6 disk.  That one was a
pocket calculator with the four operations, bitwise and, or and
exclusive or, shifts, a radix, and a print, and nothing else.  The
page in /usr/man/man1 describes it, and three of its tokens do not
mean here what they meant there: its
.bd
<
and
.bd
>
were shifts and here they compare, its
.bd
^
was exclusive or and here it is a power, and its apostrophe, which
pushed the ASCII value of the character after it, is gone.  The
example on that page that uses the apostrophe,
.bd
8 o 'N p
, does not run here.  Nor is there an
.bd
n
to print without a newline.  Two printings differ as well: in base
sixteen this dc writes F3 where the small one wrote f3, and in bases
above sixteen it prints each digit in decimal, which is what v7's dc
has always done.
.sp
An
.bd
f
prints the stack and not the registers, whatever the v7 manual says
of it; the source has always printed the stack alone.
.sp
A command line argument that begins with a minus sign is swallowed
and the next argument becomes the file, and with no argument at all
dc reads the standard input.
.sp
.bd
Y
is a debugging aid left in the source.  It dumps the top of the
stack, its pointers and the allocation counters, and is not a command
of the calculator.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
bc (1), the preprocessor for dc that takes infix expressions.
